WEBVTT
TITLE Myhill–Nerode and Distinguishability: The Classes That Are the States — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A state is what the machine remembers. After a it remembers one odd a; after aa it remembers none.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
So ask the question the machine cannot ask: does any ending z treat these two strings differently?

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
x is equivalent to y when, for every ending z, xz is in L exactly when yz is in L.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
It is an equivalence relation, and it is right-invariant: x ≈ y implies xa ≈ ya. That second property is what makes classes into states.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The signature of x is the set of endings z with xz in L. Two strings are equivalent exactly when their signatures are equal.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Endings of length at most two: a accepts only a, and b accepts only b. The two signatures already differ.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
The empty string accepts the empty ending, aa and bb. The string ab accepts ab and ba. Four signatures, four classes.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
A machine with n states is settled by endings shorter than n, so a bound of two already decides this four-state language.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Four classes for even a’s and even b’s: the empty string, a, b, and ab. Every other string joins one of them.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Each class is wholly inside or wholly outside L, never both. The empty string’s class is inside; the class of a is outside.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Name the classes by what they remember: even-even, odd-even, even-odd, odd-odd. The names are the four states.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The index of a language is its number of classes. Here the index is four, and the four-state machine has four states.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
If the index is finite, the classes are the states of a machine. The start class is the class of the empty string.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The transition is the only one available: reading a in the class of x lands in the class of xa. Right-invariance makes it well defined.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
A class is accepting when its members are in L. The four classes give four states, and the model checks the machine on every string up to length seven.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The other direction: any machine with n states gives at most n classes, because each state has one right language.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Every machine for L needs at least as many states as the index. That is the lower bound, and it is exact.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The machine built from the classes has exactly that many, so the smallest machine is the quotient, up to renaming states.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
Three languages, three indexes: four for the parity language, three for binary value divisible by three, three for ending in 01.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
So a question that asks for the minimum number of states is asking for an index, and the answer is an exact count rather than a drawing.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
GATE CSE 1997 Q20: the number of a’s divisible by two and the number of b’s divisible by three.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Each class records one remainder for a and one for b: two times three is six classes, so six states.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
GATE CSE 2011 Q42: a to the n k with k greater than zero. For n equal to three the index is four, not three.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The procedure: name the invariant a class remembers, count the values it can take, and check that every value is reachable.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
For a to the n b to the n, the strings a, aa, aaa are pairwise distinguishable: b to the i separates a to the i from every longer run.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
So every a to the i is in a class of its own, and there are infinitely many of them. The index is infinite.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
The model counts the classes among a to the nought up to a to the M as M grows: the count keeps rising.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
This is a positive proof of non-regularity: exhibit the classes, and the impossibility of a finite machine follows.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
A language that satisfies the pumping lemma: a to the i b to the j c to the k, with j equal to k whenever i is one.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
With pumping length two, every string up to length nine can be pumped: the model finds no counterexample at all.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
And it is still not regular: its intersection with a b-star c-star is exactly a b to the j c to the j, which has infinite index.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
So the pumping lemma is necessary and not sufficient. Distinguishability is both, and that is the difference between the two tests.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Distinguishability is defined on strings and needs no machine. State equivalence is defined on the states of one machine.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
On a machine, table filling computes state equivalence in a finite number of rounds. Its classes are the right languages of the states.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
And "the same outgoing letters" is not equivalence: two states can share every label and accept different endings.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
The minimal deterministic machine is unique. The minimal non-deterministic one is not: several smallest machines can accept the same language.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
What the theorem answers: the minimum number of states, exactly, and whether a language is regular at all.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
What it does not answer: it does not build a small machine for you, and it gives no bound on how large the index is.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Four indexes from this page: four for parity, three for divisibility by three, three for ending in 01, eight for the third symbol from the end.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
One sentence to keep: the states of the smallest machine are the classes of strings that no ending can tell apart.

